118 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 103 0 R
/E (The collection is based on a traditional Kyrgyz ritual called "Emchektesh," through which people could become "milk brothers or sisters." A mother would nurse both her own child and their friend. This bond was considered as strong as blood kinship; it helped prevent wars and conflicts - since one could never raise a hand against a brother - and the siblings would support each other throughout their lives.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
119 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 103 0 R
/T (Today, this custom is no longer practiced, and human connections in general have grown distant. More and more people lean toward the mindset of "I owe nothing to anyone," forgetting that humans are social beings who cannot survive in isolation. The goal of this collection is to reinterpret that direct, nurturing bond - symbolized by milk - within modern realities.)
/E (Today, this custom is no longer practiced, and human connections in general have grown distant. More and more people lean toward the mindset of "I owe nothing to anyone," forgetting that humans are social beings who cannot survive in isolation. The goal of this collection is to reinterpret that direct, nurturing bond - symbolized by milk - within modern realities.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
